data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"**My recommendation:** keep `mode` as an explicit argument to `alerts.ts`. Read `emergencyMode` from `config.ts` once, in the worker entrypoint, and pass it in. Don't have `alerts.ts` import the config itself.\n\n**Why:**\n- **Easier to test:** you can test alert behavior by passing a value. No module mocking, no changing env vars, no resetting the import cache.\n- **Picks up changes:** if `emergencyMode` can change while the worker is running (reloads, per-job overrides), importing config directly can leave alerts holding an old value.\n- **Less coupling:** alerts stays a pure policy module. You avoid the config being read as a side effect of importing alerts, and you avoid circular imports.\n- **Flexible:** a caller can force emergency behavior, for example during a manual escalation, without touching global config.\n\nIf passing `mode` through many layers gets noisy, group it into one small typed argument like `AlertContext`. Don't switch to a global import.\n\n**Test strategy:**\n- **Alerts unit tests:** run each alert path once with `emergencyMode` on and once off. Cover severity, routing and suppression in both modes. Plain inputs only, no mocks.\n- **Config tests:** check how `emergencyMode` is parsed: default when unset, valid values, and invalid values (fail fast or fall back, whichever you decide).\n- **One wiring test:** confirm the entrypoint passes `config.emergencyMode` into alerts, so the injection can't silently break.\n- **Guardrail (optional):** add a lint or import rule that blocks `alerts.ts` from importing `config.ts`."}}

